1790668508.071	A bicycle gear system, often referred to as a drivetrain, is essentially a mechanical transmission that allows the rider to efficiently manage pedaling effort across varying terrain and speeds. At its core, it consists of several interconnected components: the **crankset** (the pedals and attached chainrings at the front), the **chain**, and the **cassette/freewheel** (the cluster of sprockets on the rear wheel). The function of this system is to change the *gear ratio*—the relationship between how many times you pedal versus how fast your rear wheel turns.
1790668508.071	
1790668508.071	The magic happens when you shift gears. By moving a derailleur mechanism, the chain is moved from one set of front chainrings to another, or more commonly, it's shifted across different sized cogs on the rear cassette. A **low gear** (a small front ring paired with a large rear cog) provides high torque and allows you to pedal easily up steep hills, even if your speed is low. Conversely, a **high gear** (a large front ring paired with a small rear cog) requires more effort but allows for very fast pedaling when descending or on flat ground at speed.
1790668508.071	
